<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Ouarsenis</h1><p class="st-meta">480 CE – 580 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An obscure Berber kingdom of the Ouarsenis mountains in Algeria, known mainly from its monumental Djedar tombs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Roman and Vandal authority faded in the interior of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a> in the late 5th century, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdom emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouarsenis">Ouarsenis</a> mountains and high plains around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tiaret</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> mentions a ruler named Mastigas who, in the 530s, held most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Mauretania</a> except the coastal city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Very little is known of its government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djedars">Djedars</a>, thirteen great stepped pyramid tombs near Frenda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tiaret</a>, built between the 5th and 7th centuries, are attributed to its rulers. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions and Christian symbols show a Romanised, Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> elite ruling over pastoral and farming tribes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom disappears from the record by the late 6th century, and its lands later came under the Arab conquerors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Islamic states such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rustamid-imamate">Rustamid imamate</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a> lay close by.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-ouarsenis.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Ouarsenis" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: Naslechat · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romano-Berber_Kingdoms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>480 CE – 580 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mastigas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Algeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Mastigas, Kingdom of the Ouarsenis-Hodna</dd></dl></aside></div>
